1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,741
Look down at your keyboard.

2
00:00:02,791 --> 00:00:03,994
Q, W, E, R, T, Y.

3
00:00:04,044 --> 00:00:08,860
Billions of people type on this
arrangement every day: on laptops,

4
00:00:08,910 --> 00:00:12,251
on office desks, on the
glass of their phones.

5
00:00:12,701 --> 00:00:16,741
And yet it was designed in the
1870s, for a machine with no screen,

6
00:00:16,791 --> 00:00:19,549
no electricity, and no
idea what an email was.

7
00:00:19,998 --> 00:00:23,361
So why, after a century and a
half of technological revolution,

8
00:00:23,411 --> 00:00:24,553
are we still using it?

9
00:00:24,603 --> 00:00:26,936
The real answer is
stranger, and more human,

10
00:00:26,986 --> 00:00:28,941
than the story you've probably heard.

11
00:00:30,791 --> 00:00:34,226
Our story begins in Milwaukee,
Wisconsin, in the late 1860s.

12
00:00:34,276 --> 00:00:38,816
A newspaper editor and politician named
Christopher Latham Sholes was tinkering

13
00:00:38,866 --> 00:00:43,114
in a machine shop with two collaborators,
Carlos Glidden and Samuel Soule.

14
00:00:43,564 --> 00:00:48,106
Their early typewriter, patented in
1868, looked surprisingly like a piano.

15
00:00:48,156 --> 00:00:52,638
Two rows of keys, with the letters laid
out in roughly alphabetical order.

16
00:00:53,088 --> 00:00:54,312
It didn't stay that way.

17
00:00:54,362 --> 00:00:57,974
Over the next five years, backed by
an investor named James Densmore,

18
00:00:58,024 --> 00:01:01,637
Sholes rebuilt the machine again and
again, and the keys kept moving.

19
00:01:02,087 --> 00:01:04,165
In 1873, the design was sold to E.

20
00:01:04,215 --> 00:01:08,671
Remington and Sons, a company better
known for guns and sewing machines.

21
00:01:08,721 --> 00:01:12,176
The Sholes and Glidden
Type-Writer went on sale in 1874.

22
00:01:12,226 --> 00:01:14,053
It typed only capital letters,

23
00:01:14,103 --> 00:01:18,622
and its keyboard was already remarkably
close to the one in front of you.

24
00:01:19,072 --> 00:01:20,989
A few keys still shuffled around,

25
00:01:21,039 --> 00:01:24,148
and in 1878 the Remington
Number 2 added a shift key,

26
00:01:24,198 --> 00:01:27,248
so one machine could type
both upper and lower case.

27
00:01:27,298 --> 00:01:30,109
QWERTY, more or less as
we know it, had arrived.

28
00:01:31,359 --> 00:01:33,611
Now, here's the story
you've probably heard.

29
00:01:33,661 --> 00:01:36,436
QWERTY was deliberately
designed to slow typists down,

30
00:01:36,486 --> 00:01:37,900
so the machine wouldn't jam.

31
00:01:38,351 --> 00:01:40,016
There's a grain of truth in it.

32
00:01:40,066 --> 00:01:41,786
Early typewriters used typebars:

33
00:01:41,836 --> 00:01:44,441
metal arms arranged in a
ring beneath the paper.

34
00:01:44,491 --> 00:01:46,930
Press a key, and its arm
swings up to strike.

35
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:51,134
Press two neighbouring keys too quickly,
and the arms can collide and stick.

36
00:01:51,583 --> 00:01:54,700
So separating some common letter
pairs may well have helped.

37
00:01:54,750 --> 00:01:55,598
But look closely.

38
00:01:55,648 --> 00:01:59,663
E and R sit side by side, and E R is one
of the most common pairs in English.

39
00:01:59,713 --> 00:02:03,252
If the goal was maximum separation,
QWERTY does a strange job of it.

40
00:02:03,703 --> 00:02:07,146
And the idea of slowing typists
down makes little commercial sense.

41
00:02:07,196 --> 00:02:08,606
Remington was selling speed.

42
00:02:08,656 --> 00:02:11,839
Nobody markets a machine by
promising it will make you slower.

43
00:02:12,288 --> 00:02:14,908
Some researchers point
somewhere else entirely.

44
00:02:14,958 --> 00:02:15,817
In a 2011 paper,

45
00:02:15,867 --> 00:02:20,304
Koichi and Motoko Yasuoka of Kyoto
University argued that the layout was shaped

46
00:02:20,354 --> 00:02:23,031
by the typewriter's earliest
professional users,

47
00:02:23,081 --> 00:02:25,530
telegraph operators
transcribing Morse code,

48
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:27,972
whose needs nudged keys
into new positions.

49
00:02:28,423 --> 00:02:29,881
That account is debated too.

50
00:02:29,931 --> 00:02:33,651
The honest answer is that QWERTY
wasn't the product of one grand plan.

51
00:02:33,701 --> 00:02:37,528
It came from mechanical limits, feedback
from users, and years of small,

52
00:02:37,578 --> 00:02:38,713
practical compromises.

53
00:02:38,763 --> 00:02:41,567
It was never designed to be
the best possible layout.

54
00:02:41,617 --> 00:02:42,644
It just had to work.

55
00:02:43,894 --> 00:02:46,320
So how did a compromise
become a global standard?

56
00:02:46,370 --> 00:02:47,685
The first reason is simple.

57
00:02:47,735 --> 00:02:50,414
Remington got there early,
and sold a lot of machines.

58
00:02:50,864 --> 00:02:54,334
As typewriters spread into offices,
typing became a profession,

59
00:02:54,384 --> 00:02:57,574
and schools taught the layout
that businesses were buying.

60
00:02:57,624 --> 00:03:00,535
In a widely publicised contest
in Cincinnati in 1888,

61
00:03:00,585 --> 00:03:04,334
a stenographer named Frank McGurrin,
typing by touch on a Remington,

62
00:03:04,384 --> 00:03:06,513
beat a rival using a different machine.

63
00:03:06,563 --> 00:03:07,965
It was superb advertising.

64
00:03:08,415 --> 00:03:12,589
Then, in 1893, several of the biggest
manufacturers, including Remington,

65
00:03:12,639 --> 00:03:15,251
combined to form the
Union Typewriter Company,

66
00:03:15,301 --> 00:03:18,318
and QWERTY became the standard
across their machines.

67
00:03:18,768 --> 00:03:20,056
From there, it fed itself.

68
00:03:20,106 --> 00:03:22,577
Typists learned QWERTY
because employers used it.

69
00:03:22,627 --> 00:03:25,047
Employers bought QWERTY
because typists knew it.

70
00:03:25,097 --> 00:03:27,466
Schools taught it because
everyone demanded it.

71
00:03:27,516 --> 00:03:31,016
Economists call this a network effect:
the more people use something,

72
00:03:31,066 --> 00:03:33,383
the more valuable it
becomes to everyone else.

73
00:03:33,833 --> 00:03:37,391
When typewriters gave way to
teleprinters, then computer terminals,

74
00:03:37,441 --> 00:03:39,545
then personal computers and smartphones,

75
00:03:39,595 --> 00:03:42,076
each new technology
inherited the old keyboard.

76
00:03:42,126 --> 00:03:44,338
Not because engineers proved it was ideal,

77
00:03:44,388 --> 00:03:47,838
but because millions of fingers
already knew where the keys were.

78
00:03:48,288 --> 00:03:48,731
In 1985,

79
00:03:48,781 --> 00:03:53,471
the economist Paul David made QWERTY his
headline example of path dependence.

80
00:03:53,521 --> 00:03:58,212
That is, how an early, almost accidental
choice can lock in an entire future.

81
00:03:59,462 --> 00:04:01,259
Which raises the obvious question.

82
00:04:01,309 --> 00:04:02,292
Could we do better?

83
00:04:02,342 --> 00:04:02,726
In 1936,

84
00:04:02,776 --> 00:04:06,910
education professor August Dvorak and
his colleague William Dealey patented a

85
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:09,301
layout built on studies
of letter frequency.

86
00:04:09,351 --> 00:04:12,724
It puts all five vowels under the
left hand's resting position,

87
00:04:12,774 --> 00:04:15,713
and many of the most common
consonants under the right.

88
00:04:16,163 --> 00:04:19,778
For decades, Dvorak's layout was
promoted as dramatically faster.

89
00:04:19,828 --> 00:04:24,119
But some of the most striking early
evidence came from studies Dvorak himself

90
00:04:24,169 --> 00:04:25,021
was involved in.

91
00:04:25,071 --> 00:04:29,306
A 1956 study for the US government found
that retraining experienced typists

92
00:04:29,356 --> 00:04:30,885
didn't pay off, and in 1990,

93
00:04:30,935 --> 00:04:35,170
economists Stan Liebowitz and Stephen
Margolis argued the superiority claims

94
00:04:35,220 --> 00:04:36,298
had been overstated.

95
00:04:36,748 --> 00:04:40,600
Today, many Dvorak users report less
finger movement and greater comfort.

96
00:04:40,650 --> 00:04:43,113
But solid evidence for
big speed gains is thin,

97
00:04:43,163 --> 00:04:46,106
and for most people the
difference appears to be modest.

98
00:04:46,556 --> 00:04:49,753
Then there's Colemak, released
in 2006 by Shai Coleman.

99
00:04:49,803 --> 00:04:51,289
It's a gentler compromise.

100
00:04:51,339 --> 00:04:55,717
It moves seventeen keys, keeps shortcuts
like Z, X, C and V where they are,

101
00:04:55,767 --> 00:05:00,145
and aims to reduce finger travel without
forcing you to start from scratch.

102
00:05:00,595 --> 00:05:02,086
And that's the real trade-off.

103
00:05:02,136 --> 00:05:04,294
A better layout might
save you some effort.

104
00:05:04,344 --> 00:05:07,067
But switching means weeks of
slow, frustrating typing,

105
00:05:07,117 --> 00:05:10,097
keyboards that don't match at
work or on a friend's laptop,

106
00:05:10,147 --> 00:05:12,973
and muscle memory that fights
you every step of the way.

107
00:05:13,023 --> 00:05:14,154
The cost arrives today.

108
00:05:14,204 --> 00:05:15,951
The benefit is small, and far away.

109
00:05:17,202 --> 00:05:19,439
Meanwhile, the ground is already shifting.

110
00:05:19,489 --> 00:05:23,414
On phones, autocorrect, swipe typing
and voice input do much of the work.

111
00:05:23,464 --> 00:05:27,498
The layout matters less when you're
barely pressing individual keys at all.

112
00:05:27,948 --> 00:05:29,916
So, will we ever move beyond QWERTY?

113
00:05:29,966 --> 00:05:31,542
Probably not by replacing it.

114
00:05:31,592 --> 00:05:36,028
More likely, we'll gradually type less,
and QWERTY will fade the way it arrived:

115
00:05:36,078 --> 00:05:38,439
slowly, and without
anyone really deciding.

116
00:05:38,888 --> 00:05:42,540
QWERTY survived not because it was
perfect, but because it was good enough,

117
00:05:42,590 --> 00:05:44,366
and because everyone already knew it.

118
00:05:44,416 --> 00:05:45,550
Which leaves a question.

119
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:48,018
What else in your life
works exactly the same way?

120
00:05:48,068 --> 00:05:49,202
Tell us in the comments.

121
00:05:49,252 --> 00:05:50,880
This has been The Unnecessary Lab.

122
00:05:50,930 --> 00:05:51,867
Thanks for watching.
